WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.620
Relational databases still dominate many organizations.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.430
They're used as the backbone for online transactions.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:16.990
Data warehouses are still seen by many, as
the cornerstone of enterprise analytics.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.660
Relational databases have had a good run,
now newer applications have challenges that

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.900
exceed this relational model.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:29.199
Often, a data science team needs a more flexible
way to store their data.

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:33.140
Remember that relational databases rely on
a schema.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.079
You need to know a lot about your data before
you put it into the database.

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:39.930
So, you need to plan ahead.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:45.309
You have to know if your data is an audio
file or a text file or even video.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:48.180
Then you'll organize these fields into tables.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.770
Finally, the tables need relationships.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.350
Think about a website that sells running shoes.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.379
Imagine that you're a customer and you found
a shoe.

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:01.840
Now the website joins your shoe to an address.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.059
Now you're ready to check out on the order
page.

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:09.360
That one page needs access to four different
database tables.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.720
The shoe table, the customer table, the address
table, and finally, the shipping table.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.159
That's a lot of work for a transactional database.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:22.610
The harder your database works, the slower
it will make your website.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.000
It's also difficult to speed things up.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.010
Do you need to buy a bigger server, or do
you try to split your tables on several servers,

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:33.909
or you have several servers that synchronize
across the network?

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:39.230
When you're talking about really large websites,
these options start to seem cumbersome.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:45.060
Now imagine a database that stores everything
in one checkout page as one transaction.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:51.070
You create a record for your shoe, your customer,
their address and shipping, all in one transaction.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:55.020
You don't have to worry about splitting up
the data into tables.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.650
You don't have to worry about creating relationships.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.930
Just dump it in and you're done.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.479
That's the idea behind NoSQL.

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:09.900
NoSQL was first used as a Twitter hashtag
for developers who wanted to move beyond relational

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.290
databases.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.670
It's actually not a slam against SQL.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:19.260
In fact, NoSQL doesn't have very much to do
with SQL at all.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.209
It's about the limitations of the relational
database model.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:29.070
In general, a NoSQL database should be nonrelational,
schemaless, cluster friendly and hopefully,

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.970
open source.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.069
All of these qualities should appeal to a
data science team.

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:39.870
When a database isn't relational, it's easier
to change and simpler to use.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:43.780
There doesn't have to be a big difference
between how your web application works and

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.849
the way you store data in your database.

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:51.150
You won't have to go through the ugly process
of creating and splitting tables that already

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.130
exist, to create different views.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:58.220
This is commonly referred to as normalizing
your database.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.600
Without a schema, you don't have to worry
about knowing everything up front.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.330
Let's say the website was bought by a larger
company.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.830
This company wants to add your customers to
their frequent buyer program.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:15.730
With a relational database, this is a serious
architectural challenge.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.880
Should you have the frequent buyer identified
in the customer table?

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.150
Maybe you need to create a whole new table
of frequent buyer numbers.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.480
Can a customer have more than one buyer number?

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.810
Can two customers share the same buyer number?

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.340
All this needs to be sorted out.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:38.110
You have to re-work the database and figure
out how to correct for missing data.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:42.239
Without a schema, any new fields become almost
trivial.

00:03:42.239 --> 00:03:45.220
You can just store it as one transaction.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.519
If the customer has a frequent buyer number,
then it's loaded as part of the transaction.

00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:53.379
If they don't, then the field doesn't exist.

00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:58.030
Finally, a NoSQL database should be cluster-friendly.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:03.440
You should be able to store the data in several
hundred, or even thousand database servers.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:09.049
In a NoSQL database, the records saved in
a transaction is called an aggregate.

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:11.879
These aggregates hold all of the data.

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:16.100
They have the shoe, the customer address and
shipping information.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:20.979
These aggregates are easier to synchronize
across many database servers.

00:04:20.979 --> 00:04:23.890
Many of the servers work in clusters.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:28.410
That way they can synchronize and then send
out their updates to other clusters.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:31.380
The word cluster should sound familiar.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.880
It's also how Hadoop works with its data sets.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:41.060
In fact, much of Hadoop is built on HBase,
which is an open source NoSQL database.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:45.100
When you're working in a data science team,
you're almost certainly going to run into

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.890
NoSQL.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:51.960
For many organizations, this is the best way
to deal with large data sets.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:58.139
Because of its simpler design, it's also easier
for developers to create web applications.

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:00.479
These applications can quickly grow to an
enterprise scale.

